PROBLEM 3 • Intermediate
Backyard Toolshed Routine
A gardening app models tools as a family: every GardenTool can be used, and a Pruner is a cutting tool with its own
extra trimming step layered on top of the general cutting-tool routine.
Requirements
● Pruner must extend CuttingTool, and CuttingTool must extend GardenTool — a genuine multilevel hierarchy
three classes deep, with each subclass constructor calling its parent's constructor using super(...).
● GardenTool must be abstract with an abstract use() method; CuttingTool must override use() with its own
concrete version; Pruner must override use() again.
● Each override (in CuttingTool and Pruner) must call super.use() first and then add its own extra detail.
Function Signature(s)
public abstract class GardenTool()
public abstract String use();
public CuttingTool() // overrides use(), calls super.use()
public Pruner() // overrides use() again, calls super.use()
Examples
Input Output
CuttingTool c = new CuttingTool();
c.use()

"Using the tool in the garden,
blade sharpened first"

Input Output
Pruner p = new Pruner();
p.use()

"Using the tool in the garden,
blade sharpened first, then
trimming branches precisely"
Explanation: Pruner reuses CuttingTool's own message via super.use() and appends its own detail, the same reuse pattern working
one level or two levels deep.
HINT Each use() override should call super.use() first, then add only the new detail that level introduces.
CONSTRAINTS: No arguments are required to construct any of the three classes.

